and, stepping on Pieria and lovely Emathia,
she rushed to the snowy mountains of the horse-herding Thracians,
the topmost peaks;

“Sleep, lord both of all gods and of all men,
both once indeed my word you heard, and still also now
obey;

But he, waking up, was growing harsh,
flinging the gods about the house, and me beyond all others
he sought;

There Sleep stayed, before the eyes of Zeus to look on,
having climbed into a very tall fir, which then on Ida,
being the tallest that had grown, reached through the mist to the aether;

“I am going to see the ends of the much-nourishing earth,
and Oceanus, the origin of the gods, and mother Tethys,
who in their own houses reared me well and fostered me;
